e following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary  \n\n**Verdict.**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 1\\le k\\le n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition exists **iff** at least one of the lines is non‑sunny; any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) can be achieved.\n\n**Method Sketch.**  \n\n1. **Impossibility of \\(k=0\\).**  \n   *Show that with only sunny lines one needs at least \\(2n-1\\) lines to cover all points, whereas we are allowed only \\(n\\) lines.*  \n   - Any sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   - By a simple affine change of coordinates we may assume that all sunny lines have slope \\(1\\) (rotate the picture).  \n   - A line of slope \\(1\\) contains exactly the points with a fixed value of the difference \\(b-a\\).  \n   - The set \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) realizes all integer differences from \\(-(n-1)\\) to \\(n-1\\); there are \\(2n-1\\) of them.  \n   - Hence at least \\(2n-1\\) distinct slope‑\\(1\\) lines are required, contradicting the bound \\(n\\).  \n   Therefore \\(k=0\\) is impossible.\n\n2. **Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n   *Use \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) to cover a central “band’’ of the triangle and use \\(n-k\\) horizontal (non‑sunny) lines to cover the remaining rows.*  \n   - Choose the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n     \\[\n     L_i:\\; y = x + (i-1),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n     \\]\n     Their slopes are \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); thus they are sunny and distinct.  \n   - These lines cover all points with  \n     \\[\n     0\\le b-a\\le k-1 .\n     \\]\n   - The points that are still uncovered are those with either \\(b-a<0\\) (i.e. \\(b<a\\)) or \\(b-a\\ge k\\).  \n   - For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-k\\) draw the horizontal line  \n     \\[\n     H_t:\\; y = t .\n     \\]\n     These \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny (horizontal) and distinct.\n\n   - **Verification that every required point is covered.**  \n     Let \\((a,b)\\) satisfy \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n     *If \\(b\\le n-k\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).  \n     *If \\(b> n-k\\)*, set \\(b' = b-(n-k)\\). Then \\(b'\\ge1\\) and\n     \\[\n     a+b' = a+b-(n-k) \\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1 .\n     \\]\n     Hence \\(a+b'\\le k+1\\), i.e. \\(b'-a\\) belongs to \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  \n     Consequently \\((a,b)= (a,b'+n-k)\\) lies on the sunny line\n     \\[\n     L_{\\,b'-a+1}:\\; y = x + (b'-a) + (n-k) .\n     \\]\n     Thus every point of the required set belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n   - The total number of lines is \\(k\\) sunny + \\((n-k)\\) non‑sunny = \\(n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   The impossibility of \\(k=0\\) together with the explicit construction for every \\(k\\ge1\\) shows that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Non‑existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covers \\(S_n\\).\nApply a non‑singular affine transformation that sends the three forbidden\ndirections to the coordinate axes; after this transformation every sunny line\nhas slope \\(1\\).  (For instance, rotate the plane by \\(45^\\circ\\) and then\nscale the axes.)\n\nHence we may suppose that each line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) is of the form  \n\n\\[\ny = x + c,\\qquad c\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\]\n\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\) we have \\(b-a=c\\) exactly when the point lies on the line\nwith intercept \\(c\\).  Consequently a single line of slope \\(1\\) can cover only\nthose points of \\(S_n\\) that share the same integer difference \\(b-a\\).\n\nThe set of differences that actually occur in \\(S_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\nD_n=\\{\\,b-a\\mid (a,b)\\in S_n\\,\\}\n      =\\{-(n-1),-(n-2),\\dots ,0,\\dots ,n-2,n-1\\},\n\\]\nwhich contains \\(2n-1\\) distinct integers.  Therefore at least \\(2n-1\\) distinct\nlines of slope \\(1\\) are necessary to cover all points of \\(S_n\\).  Since we are\nrestricted to only \\(n\\) lines, a covering with **no** non‑sunny line is\nimpossible.\n\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ is impossible for every }n\\ge3 } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  We exhibit a family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) of \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines that covers \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.1  Sunny lines  \n\nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x + (i-1).\n\\]\n\nAll \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence they are sunny and are pairwise\ndistinct because their intercepts differ.\n\n#### 2.2  Non‑sunny (horizontal) lines  \n\nFor \\(t=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nH_t:\\; y = t .\n\\]\n\nThese are horizontal, therefore non‑sunny, and distinct.\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k = \\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{\\,n-k}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines,\nhence \\(n\\) lines in total.\n\n#### 2.3  Verification of the covering property  \n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(b\\le n-k\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(b> n-k\\).*  \nWrite \\(b = b'+(n-k)\\) with \\(b'\\ge1\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\na+b' = a+b-(n-k) \\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(a+b'\\le k+1\\), which is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n0\\le b'-a\\le k-1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently there exists a unique integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) such that\n\\(b'-a = i-1\\).  For this \\(i\\),\n\n\\[\nb = b'+(n-k) = a + (i-1) + (n-k),\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x + (i-1) + (n-k).\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_i\\).\n\nIn both cases the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered by a line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\nSince \\((a,b)\\) was arbitrary, \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) covers the whole set \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof  \n\nWe have proved:\n\n* No configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists (Section 1).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration with exactly\n  \\(k\\) sunny lines exists (Section 2).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the final answer is:**  \n*For any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are exactly the\nintegers \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).*\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
