ains \\(2n-1\\) distinct integers.\n\nA non‑sunny line can be of three types:\n\n* horizontal \\(y=c\\) – it contains all points with \\(b=c\\), i.e. all\n  differences \\(c-a\\) for \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\);\n* vertical \\(x=c\\) – it contains all points with \\(a=c\\), i.e. all\n  differences \\(b-c\\) for \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\);\n* slope \\(-1\\) line \\(x+y=c\\) – it contains all points with\n  \\(a+b=c\\), i.e. all differences \\(b-a\\) ranging from\n  \\(-(c-2)\\) to \\(c-2\\).\n\nIn any case a single non‑sunny line can cover **at most** \\(n-1\\) distinct\ndifferences (the maximal number is attained by the horizontal line\n\\(y=1\\) or the vertical line \\(x=1\\)).  Consequently, after using all\n\\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines we can cover at most \\((n-k)(n-1)\\) differences,\nbut each of those lines actually covers at most \\(n-1\\) *different*\ndifferences, so the total number of distinct differences covered by the\nnon‑sunny lines is at most \\(n-1\\).\n\nHence the remaining uncovered differences are at least  \n\n\\[\n|D_n|-(n-1)= (2n-1)-(n-1)=n .\n\\]\n\nEach of those remaining differences requires a distinct sunny line, so we\nmust have  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge n-(n-1)=\\left\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\right\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nRe‑arranging gives the upper bound  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\right\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Lemma 3 (realisation of every admissible \\(k\\)).*  \nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\).\nConstruct the family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) as follows:\n\n1. the horizontal line \\(H_1:\\;y=1\\) (non‑sunny);\n2. for \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\) the sunny line \\(L_i:\\;y=x+i\\);\n3. for \\(j=2,3,\\dots ,n-k\\) the horizontal lines \\(H_j:\\;y=j\\).\n\nThe family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines,\nhence \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le n-k\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n*If \\(b> n-k\\)*, write \\(b=b'+(n-k)\\) with \\(b'\\ge1\\). 