"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to obtain a complete, rigorous classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following partial result.\n\n> **Partial Theorem.**  \n> Let \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n> *For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*  \n> Consequently the set of possible values of \\(k\\) contains the whole interval \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n> (Whether any further restriction on \\(k\\) exists remains open.)\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\).**  \n   *Idea.* Use \\(k\\) horizontal (non\u2011sunny) lines to cover the lowest \\(k\\) rows of the lattice triangle, and cover the remaining points (which form a smaller triangle of side\u2011length \\(n-k\\)) with \\(n-k\\) lines of slope \\(1\\). Since a line of slope \\(1\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), each of these \\(n-k\\) lines is sunny.  \n\n   *Details.*  \n   - For each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le k\\) draw the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \n   - The points that are still uncovered are precisely  \n     \\[\n     T_{n-k}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1,\\; b>k\\}.\n     \\]\n     After the change of variables \\(a'=a,\\; b'=b-k\\) this set is in bijection with  \n     \\[\n     S_{n-k}=\\{(a',b')\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a'+b'\\le (n-k)+1\\},\n     \\]\n     i.e. a right\u2011angled lattice triangle of side\u2011length \\(n-k\\).  \n   - For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-k\\) draw the line of slope \\(1\\) given by  \n     \\[\n     L_c:\\; y = x + (c-1).\n     \\]\n     The points of \\(S_{n-k}\\) lie on exactly one of these lines, because for any\n     \\((a',b')\\in S_{n-k}\\) we have \\(b'-a' = c-1\\) for a unique \\(c\\) in the stated range.  \n   - Translating back to the original coordinates, the lines \\(L_c\\) become the\n     lines \\(y = x + (c-1)+k\\), which are still of slope \\(1\\) and therefore sunny.  \n\n   The total number of lines is \\(k\\) horizontal (non\u2011sunny) plus \\((n-k)\\) slope\u2011\\(1\\) (sunny), i.e. \\(n\\) lines, and exactly \\(n-k\\) of them are sunny.  Since \\(k\\) was arbitrary, we obtain a configuration with any prescribed number of sunny lines between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).\n\n2. **Why the construction works for every \\(k\\).**  \n   - The horizontal lines are pairwise distinct and clearly not sunny.  \n   - The slope\u2011\\(1\\) lines are pairwise distinct, have slope \\(1\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\), and therefore are sunny.  \n   - Every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) either satisfies \\(b\\le k\\) (hence lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\)) or satisfies \\(b>k\\); in the latter case, writing \\(b'=b-k\\) we have \\(a+b' \\le (n-k)+1\\), so \\((a,b')\\in S_{n-k}\\) and consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y = x + (b-a)+k\\), which is one of the slope\u2011\\(1\\) lines.  \n\nThus the construction indeed covers all required points and yields exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for an arbitrary integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\n1. **Horizontal (non\u2011sunny) lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   H_b:\\; y=b .\n   \\]\n\n   The \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{H_b\\}_{b=1}^{k}\\) are distinct, horizontal, and therefore\n   non\u2011sunny.\n\n2. **Remaining points.**  \n   After removing the points lying on the horizontal lines we are left with  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{n-k}= \\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid b>k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Set \\(a'=a\\) and \\(b'=b-k\\).  Because \\(b>k\\) we have \\(b'\\ge 1\\), and\n\n   \\[\n   a'+b' = a+(b-k) \\le (n+1)-k = (n-k)+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the map \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a',b')\\) is a bijection between \\(T_{n-k}\\) and  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{n-k}= \\{(a',b')\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a'+b'\\le (n-k)+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Geometrically, \\(S_{n-k}\\) is the same right\u2011angled lattice triangle as\n   \\(S_n\\) but with side\u2011length reduced from \\(n\\) to \\(n-k\\).\n\n3. **Sunny lines of slope \\(1\\).**  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-k\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_c:\\; y = x + (c-1) .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of each \\(L_c\\) is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\);\n   therefore every \\(L_c\\) is sunny.  The lines \\(\\{L_c\\}_{c=1}^{n-k}\\) are\n   distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\n   For any point \\((a',b')\\in S_{n-k}\\) we have \\(b'-a' = c-1\\) for a unique\n   integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-k\\); consequently \\((a',b')\\) lies on the\n   unique line \\(L_c\\).  Hence the family \\(\\{L_c\\}\\) covers the whole set\n   \\(S_{n-k}\\).\n\n4. **Returning to the original coordinates.**  \n   Translating the lines \\(L_c\\) back by adding \\(k\\) to the \\(y\\)-coordinate\n   gives the lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\widetilde L_c:\\; y = x + (c-1) + k ,\\qquad 1\\le c\\le n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(\\widetilde L_c\\) is still of slope \\(1\\) and therefore sunny.  Moreover,\n   for any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n-k}\\) we have \\((a',b')=(a,b-k)\\in S_{n-k}\\), so\n   \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\widetilde L_c\\) where \\(c\\) is determined by\n   \\(b'-a' = c-1\\).  Thus the lines \\(\\{\\widetilde L_c\\}\\) cover exactly the\n   points of \\(T_{n-k}\\).\n\n5. **Counting the lines.**  \n   The total family of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{F}_k = \\{H_b\\mid 1\\le b\\le k\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n                 \\{\\widetilde L_c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n-k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   It contains \\(k\\) horizontal (non\u2011sunny) lines and \\(n-k\\) slope\u2011\\(1\\)\n   (sunny) lines, altogether \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.  By construction\n   every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{F}_k\\).\n\nHence for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Consequence\n\nThe construction shows that **every** integer \\(k\\) in the interval\n\\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is realizable.  Therefore the set of admissible values of\n\\(k\\) contains this whole interval.  Whether additional restrictions on \\(k\\)\nexist (i.e. whether some values larger than \\(n\\) could be forced) remains an\nopen question; the present argument does not preclude the possibility of\nfurther constraints.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are attainable.}}\n\\]"
